\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $K\subset\mathbb C$ be compact. Write $\mathcal H(K)$ for the uniform closure in $C(K)$ of restrictions of functions analytic in some open neighborhood of $K$ (the neighborhood may depend on the function). Equivalently, $u\in\mathcal H(K)$ means there are analytic-near-$K$ functions $u_j$ with $\sup_K|u_j-u|\to0$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every $f\in C(K)$, does $f^2\in\mathcal H(K)$ imply $f\in\mathcal H(K)$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does uniform approximation by functions analytic near a compact set pass from the square of a continuous function to the function itself?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2309014 (AMR-022-9014). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory} (2018), arXiv:1809.07200. Problem 9.14, complete original question, all following updates, and the relevant chapter definitions. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
